<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
  <title>Reading Quiz 01</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      <alert>Contents:</alert> This quiz covers sections 1.1 through 1.6, and you may skip material on the cross product.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Instructions:</alert> Answer all the questions to the best of your ability and upload your work to the "Quiz 1" assignment in Canvas.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <page>
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The <q>geometric sketch</q> of the identity <m>\vec{A}+\vec{B}=\vec{B}+\vec{A}</m>on page 4 seems to assume its result: the vectors
          <m>\vec{A}</m> and <m>\vec{B}</m>are drawn in different order and different places. 
          What property of vectors allows the sketch to be made as shown and keeps the proof valid?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          	On page 5, the book shows the projection of <m>\vec{B}</m> on <m>\vec{A}</m>. Draw a sketch of the projection of <m>\vec{A}</m> on <m>\vec{B}</m> 
            and use that to establish the equations for <m>\vec{A} \cdot \vec{B}</m> in terms of projections.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          		On example 1.1, what trig identity is being used to relate <m>\cos\phi</m> and <m>\cos\theta</m>? Look it up if needed!
              How are <m>\sin\phi</m> and <m>\sin\theta</m> related? 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          	On page 9, the book writes the equation <m>\theta=\arctan(A_y/A_x)</m>. It's important to note we won't always use the angle with the x axis. 
            If we defined the angle <m>\phi</m> to be the angle with the y axis, with positive angle defined to be counterclockwise, then 
            what are the components of a vector <m>\vec{A}</m> in terms of <m>|\vec{A}|</m> and <m>\phi</m>? Be sure to include a sketch with your answer.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>
